Interesting discussion mostly ego driven and low on contents but two things stand out:

<a> IPI (inter processor interrupt) is / can be pretty expensive

<b> when you unmap ptes you may have to do the IPI. I m off the opinion you can defer it dont care if Linux does it or not
    but fact is if you defer it make sure you dont reallocate that range until all cores have claimed to have tlb invalidated
    that range.

    thats easy to do queue the invalidate range for every core that process is allowed to be scheduled on. Each time a thread
    from this process loads on a core it should check for pending invalidate commands on that core and issue all of them pending
    or some of them pending.

    if all pending invalidates for a VA range have been issued you can recycle the VA range back into the free list.

    if under pressure and you need the VA bad do the IPI there and then and take the perf penalty.

[Late thoughts] you can definitely do this for munmap but not for mprotect. for that case
I ve been thinking about a per cpu bitmask in task_struct. instead of sending an IPI to a 
processor check if this bit is set if not queue the tlb invalidate command. this would
